<h1>external index</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
An auxiliary data structure added to a main data structure to improve operations, such as a <a href="search.html"><em>search</em></a> on a secondary <a href="key.html"><em>key</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="invertedIndex.html"><em>inverted index</em></a>, <a href="hashtab.html"><em>hash table</em></a>, <a href="searchtree.html"><em>search tree</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
The implementation probably embeds parts of the external index in the primary data structure, such as adding a few fields to each <a href="node.html"><em>node</em></a>.  If items are added to or deleted from the primary data structure, the external index must be maintained, too.  Data may have multiple external indexes, such as a data base of movies with inverted indexes for title, actors, and genre.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

